KerBridge on Debian
===================

Installing the packages does not finish the deployment. It puts the programs on
the host, writes a configuration set from the answers you gave, and creates the
directories. Three things are still to come, and none of them is a thing a
package is allowed to do for you: the realm, the directory inside it, and the
client secret of your cloud IdP.

Start here
----------

    sudo kbsetup status

It reads the state on this host, says which steps are done, and prints the
command for the next one. It changes nothing, so it is safe to run at any
point. Run it again after each step.

On a host that has only kerbridge-manage, there is no kbsetup. That host needs
no realm and runs no daemon; see rsat-and-kerbridge-management.md in
/usr/share/doc/kerbridge-manage/.

The steps kbsetup status walks
------------------------------

  kbsetup realm       Provisions the Samba domain, the LDAPS CA and the
                      certificate. Everything it decides is baked into the
                      Samba database and cannot be changed by editing
                      configuration afterwards. Read the guide before this one.

  kbsetup directory   Creates the organizational units, the service accounts
                      and their delegation. Run it again after you add a cloud
                      IdP; it creates only what is absent.

  kbsetup secrets     Asks for the credentials no KerBridge command can
                      generate -- today that is your cloud IdP's client secret
                      -- and writes each one to the file the configuration set
                      names, at the mode its reader needs. The value is typed
                      with the echo off and goes straight into the file.

You then terminate TLS in front of the broker, which serves plain HTTP on the
loopback interface and refuses any wider address. Example configurations for
Caddy and nginx are in /usr/share/doc/kerbridge-broker/examples/.

The files
---------

  /etc/kerbridge/            The configuration set. Every option is one line,
                             and a line that starts with # sets nothing and
                             takes the default. The comments in each file say
                             what the option does.

  /etc/kerbridge.secrets/    Credentials, as files. generated/ holds what
                             kbsetup wrote; the rest is what you supply.
                             Nothing here is ever written by a package.

  kbconfig decisions         Every option this deployment sets, with the value
                             it would use if it set none.
  kbconfig check             Whether the set is coherent. Each daemon runs this
                             before it starts.

The install questions are asked once
------------------------------------

They are used on a first install only, to write a configuration set that is not
there yet. No package edits a configuration file that already exists.

Running `dpkg-reconfigure kerbridge-config` afterwards shows you what the files
say rather than what you answered, and then writes nothing. To change a live
deployment, edit the files.

No secret is ever asked for through debconf. A value that passes through debconf
is written to /var/cache/debconf/config.dat and again to the world-readable
config.dat-old, so the install questions ask only for a realm, a URL, public
application identifiers and a group's object id. `kbsetup secrets` collects the
rest.

Two states that are normal, and are not faults
----------------------------------------------

  kerbridge-issuerd runs idle before you provision. It starts working when
  kbsetup realm gives it a realm.

  kerbridge-broker is failed until kbsetup directory runs. Its LDAP bind
  password does not exist until then. Afterwards, clear the counter before you
  start it:

    sudo systemctl reset-failed kerbridge-broker
    sudo systemctl start kerbridge-broker

  A plain `systemctl start` inside the 60-second window is refused and does not
  even run the unit's checks, so retrying in a loop looks like a unit that is
  permanently stuck.

Uninstalling
------------

A purge is not a decommission. It removes the configuration set. It does not
remove /etc/kerbridge.secrets/, the audit records under /var/log/kerbridge/, or
anything under /etc/samba or /var/lib/samba -- so a purged host still runs the
domain controller and still holds the client secret and the realm Administrator
password on disk. To decommission a host, erase /etc/kerbridge.secrets/ as well.

